WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.544
Detection tools help find leaks earlier,

2
00:00:02.624 --> 00:00:06.070
but they work best when the code already
expresses ownership clearly.

3
00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:09.421
Compiler warnings, static analysis, leak
checkers, sanitizers,

4
00:00:09.501 --> 00:00:13.648
allocation tracing, tests, and code review
each provide a different view of the same

5
00:00:13.728 --> 00:00:14.721
lifecycle problem.

6
00:00:14.801 --> 00:00:18.773
Compiler warnings can reveal suspicious
ownership and control-flow patterns.

7
00:00:18.853 --> 00:00:21.657
Warnings around ignored results,
unreachable cleanup,

8
00:00:21.737 --> 00:00:24.131
uninitialized pointers, suspicious
branches,

9
00:00:24.211 --> 00:00:28.125
or mismatched types near allocation should
be reviewed rather than dismissed.

10
00:00:28.205 --> 00:00:31.826
A warning is often a clue that the cleanup
story is harder than it should be.

11
00:00:31.906 --> 00:00:35.586
Static analysis can help identify missed
cleanup, overwritten pointers,

12
00:00:35.666 --> 00:00:39.696
inconsistent ownership assumptions, and
paths where a function returns without

13
00:00:39.776 --> 00:00:41.353
releasing acquired resources.

14
00:00:41.433 --> 00:00:45.580
Static tools are especially useful when
allocation and cleanup happen in different

15
00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:46.244
functions.

16
00:00:46.324 --> 00:00:50.354
Leak checkers and sanitizers can expose
unreleased allocations during testing.

17
00:00:50.434 --> 00:00:54.406
They can show which allocation sites
remain live at a checkpoint or process exit,

18
00:00:54.486 --> 00:00:58.633
and they can help distinguish intentional
long-lived allocations from accidental

19
00:00:58.713 --> 00:00:59.064
leaks.

20
00:00:59.144 --> 00:01:01.889
The findings should lead to fixes and
regression tests.

21
00:01:01.969 --> 00:01:05.707
Allocation tracing can connect allocation
sites to missing release paths.

22
00:01:05.787 --> 00:01:09.876
This is useful in services and libraries
where the leak only appears after repeated

23
00:01:09.956 --> 00:01:10.248
work.

24
00:01:10.328 --> 00:01:12.430
Tracing should be interpreted carefully,

25
00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:16.774
because some allocations are intentionally
long-lived caches while others are meant

26
00:01:16.854 --> 00:01:18.665
to be released after each operation.

27
00:01:18.745 --> 00:01:22.483
Tests should exercise success paths,
failure paths, repeated operations,

28
00:01:22.563 --> 00:01:26.944
partial initialization, replacement,
removal, and cleanup after allocation failure.

29
00:01:27.024 --> 00:01:30.587
Code review should follow each allocation
through ownership transfer,

30
00:01:30.667 --> 00:01:32.477
storage, replacement, and cleanup.

31
00:01:32.557 --> 00:01:34.660
That trace is the heart of leak
prevention.
